<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  10.2 Infinite Series
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-infinite-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Infinite Series</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Define an infinite sequence, an infinite series, and the sequence of
partial sums, and decide convergence of a series from its partial sums.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recognize a geometric series, and determine whether it converges and
what it converges to.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Apply the <m>n</m>th Term Test for divergence, and understand what the
test can and cannot conclude.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Combine convergent series using the sum, difference, and constant
multiple rules, and evaluate telescoping series from their partial
sums.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
What does it mean to add infinitely many numbers together? In this section
we answer that question by defining an infinite series and its sequence of
partial sums, so that convergence of a series becomes a statement about a
limit we already know how to study. We then put the definition to work on
the geometric series, whose behavior we can settle completely, and on the
<m>n</m>th Term Test, which is our first quick check for divergence.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-series-definitions">
<title>Definitions</title>

<definition xml:id="def-sequence">
<title>Sequence</title>
<statement>
<p>
A <term>sequence</term> is a list of numbers,
<m>a_1, a_2, a_3, \cdots</m>. An <term>infinite sequence</term> of
numbers is a function whose domain is the set of positive integers.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="ex-halving-sequence">
<title>A Sequence of Halves</title>
<statement>
<p>
The numbers
<m>\dfrac12, \dfrac14, \dfrac18, \dfrac{1}{16}, \cdots</m> form an
infinite sequence; its <m>n</m>th term is
<m>a_n = \left(\dfrac12\right)^{n}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<definition xml:id="def-infinite-series">
<title>Infinite Series</title>
<statement>
<p>
The sum of the numbers in an infinite sequence <m>\{a_n\}</m>, i.e.
<m>a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \cdots = \displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m>,
is called an <term>infinite series</term>. Here <m>a_n</m> is the
<m>n</m>th term of the series.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="ex-halving-series">
<title>An Infinite Series with a Finite Sum</title>
<statement>
<p>
Infinite sequences can have finite sums. Consider the sum of the
sequence from <xref ref="ex-halving-sequence"/>, i.e.
<m>\dfrac12 + \dfrac14 + \dfrac18 + \dfrac{1}{16} + \cdots = 1</m>.
</p>
<p>
It is most convenient to evaluate the result of this sum geometrically.
The above sum corresponds to the area of a square of side one. See
<xref ref="fig-series-square"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-series-square">
<caption>
The sum <m>\frac12 + \frac14 + \frac18 + \frac{1}{16} + \cdots</m>
fills up a square of side one, so the sum is <m>1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
