</figure>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-series-partial-sums">
<title>Partial Sums</title>

<p>
Consider the infinite sequence
<m>\dfrac12, \dfrac14, \dfrac18, \dfrac{1}{16}, \cdots</m>. Let us
denote the sum of the first <m>n</m> terms in this sequence by
<m>s_n</m>, which are known as <term>partial sums</term>. Can we find a
pattern in the sequence of partial sums?

<md>
<mrow>s_1 \amp = \frac12 = 1 - \frac12</mrow>
<mrow>s_2 \amp = \frac12 + \frac14 = 1 - \frac14</mrow>
<mrow>s_3 \amp = \frac12 + \frac14 + \frac18 = 1 - \frac18</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\;\vdots</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-halving-partial-sum" number="yes">s_n \amp
= \sum_{k=1}^{n} \left(\frac12\right)^{k} = 1 - \frac{1}{2^{n}}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
From the pattern in <xref ref="eq-halving-partial-sum"/>, we can compute
the result of the infinite series by taking the limit of the partial sum
<m>s_n</m> as <m>n \to \infty</m>.

<md>
<mrow>\frac12 + \frac14 + \frac18 + \frac{1}{16} + \cdots
\amp = \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac12\right)^{k}
= \lim_{n\to\infty} s_n</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-halving-sum" number="yes">\amp
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \left(1 - \frac{1}{2^{n}}\right) = 1</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that this is the same answer that we found using our geometric
analysis (using the square).
</p>

<p>
Note that in the above example, the sequence of the partial sums
converged to <m>1</m>. In general, if the sequence of the partial sums
converges to a number, we say that the series <term>converges</term>,
otherwise we say that the series <term>diverges</term>.
</p>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-series-geometric">
<title>Geometric Series</title>

<p>
An important example of infinite series is the geometric series. The
<term>geometric series</term> is of the form
<md xml:id="eq-geometric-series" number="yes">
a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1} + \cdots
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1},
</md>
where <m>a, r</m> are real numbers and <m>a \ne 0</m>. Note that we can
re-write the series as <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} ar^{n}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Let us compute the partial sum <m>s_n</m> for the geometric series. The
partial sum is

<md xml:id="eq-series-geo-sn" number="yes">
s_n = a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
If we multiply equation <xref ref="eq-series-geo-sn"/> by <m>r</m>, we
get a similar expression.

<md xml:id="eq-series-geo-rsn" number="yes">
r s_n = ar + ar^2 + ar^3 + \cdots + ar^{n}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Then deducting <xref ref="eq-series-geo-rsn"/> from
<xref ref="eq-series-geo-sn"/> we get

<md>
<mrow>s_n - r s_n \amp = a - ar^{n}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-geometric-partial-sum" number="yes">s_n \amp
= a\,\frac{1-r^{n}}{1-r}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Next, to compute the result of the geometric series, we take the limit
of the partial sum <xref ref="eq-geometric-partial-sum"/> as in the
previous example.

<md>
<mrow>\lim_{n\to\infty} s_n \amp
= \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{a(1-r^{n})}{1-r}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{a}{1-r}
- \frac{a}{1-r}\lim_{n\to\infty} r^{n}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that if <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, we have
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} r^{n} = 0</m> and hence
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} s_n = \dfrac{a}{1-r}</m>, whereas if
<m>|r| \gt 1</m>, <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} r^{n}</m> diverges and
therefore the geometric series diverges. That leaves the two cases with
<m>|r| = 1</m>. When <m>r = 1</m>, the partial sum is

<md>
s_n = a + a + a + \cdots + a = na,
</md>
</p>

<p>
limit of which diverges.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-geometric-r-negative-one">
<title>What Happens When <m>r = -1</m>?</title>
<statement>
<p>
We have now handled <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, <m>|r| \gt 1</m>, and
<m>r = 1</m>, but one case is still missing: <m>r = -1</m>. Write out the
partial sums <m>s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4</m> of the series
<m>a - a + a - a + \cdots = \displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a(-1)^{n-1}</m>
for <m>a \neq 0</m>. Does the sequence of partial sums approach a single
number as <m>n \to \infty</m>? What does that tell you about the series?
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
The partial sums do not grow without bound here, the way they do when
<m>r = 1</m>. Look instead at whether they settle down to one value.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>

<p>
Putting all of the cases together, we can say that if <m>|r| \ge 1</m>,
the geometric series is divergent.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="summary-geometric-series">
<title>Summary</title>
<p>
The geometric series <xref ref="eq-geometric-series"/> converges to
<m>\dfrac{a}{1-r}</m> if <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-geometric-sum" number="yes">
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1} = \frac{a}{1-r},
\qquad |r| \lt 1,
</md>
and diverges if <m>|r| \ge 1</m>.
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Next, as an application of the geometric series, we will go through the
following example, which is from our textbook.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-series-ball">
<title>A bouncing ball</title>
<statement>
<p>
You drop a ball from <m>a</m> meters above a flat surface. Each time
the ball hits the surface after falling a distance <m>h</m>, it
rebounds a distance <m>rh</m>, where <m>r</m> is positive but less than
1. Find the total distance the ball travels up and down.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
As can be seen in <xref ref="fig-series-ball"/>, the total vertical
distance travelled by the ball can be written as the infinite series

<md>
a + 2ar + 2ar^2 + \cdots + 2ar^{n-1} + \cdots
</md>
</p>

<p>
which can be re-written and then summed with
<xref ref="eq-geometric-sum"/>, since <m>|r| \lt 1</m> here.

<md>
<mrow>-a + 2a \amp + 2ar + 2ar^2 + \cdots + 2ar^{n-1} + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -a + 2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1}
= -a + 2\,\frac{a}{1-r}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{-a + ar + 2a}{1-r} = \frac{a(1+r)}{1-r}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-series-ball">
<caption>
The ball falls a distance <m>a</m>, then rises and falls a distance
<m>ar</m>, then <m>ar^2</m>, and so on, so the total distance travelled
is <m>a + 2ar + 2ar^2 + \cdots</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-series-ball">
<diagram dimensions="(340,280)" margins="12">
<definition>b0(t) = (0.55 + 0.55*t, 4.0*(1 - t^2))</definition>
<definition>b1(t) = (1.10 + 0.9*t, 2.4*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b2(t) = (2.00 + 0.7*t, 1.44*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b3(t) = (2.70 + 0.55*t, 0.864*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b4(t) = (3.25 + 0.42*t, 0.5184*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.7,-0.5,4.4,4.6)">
<line at="ball-ground" endpoints="((-0.35,0),(4.2,0))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ball-wall" endpoints="((-0.35,0),(-0.35,4.4))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
