</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-series-nth-term">
<title>The <m>n</m>th Term Test</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-series-nth-term">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> converges, then
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
<term>Important note:</term> If <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = 0</m>,
we <em>cannot</em> conclude that
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> converges. See parts D and E
in the example below. Also see <xref ref="example-series-telescoping-ln"/>.
</p>

<p>
The following test is a consequence of the above theorem.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="test-nth-term">
<title>The <m>n</m>th Term Test</title>
<p>
If <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n</m> does not exist or
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n \ne 0</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> diverges.
</p>
</assemblage>

<example xml:id="example-series-convergence">
<title>Testing series for convergence</title>
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the series is convergent or divergent. If it is
convergent, find its sum.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^n</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n^3+1}{n^2+n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac49 - \dfrac{8}{27} + \dfrac{16}{81} - \dfrac{32}{243} + \cdots</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} n = \infty</m>,
so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test,
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n</m> is divergent.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} (-1)^n</m>
does not exist, so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test,
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^n</m> is divergent.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n
= \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{n^3+1}{n^2+n}
= \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{n^3}{n^2}
= \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} n = \infty</m>,
so by the <m>n</m>th Term Test,
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \dfrac{n^3+1}{n^2+n}</m> is
divergent.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Note that in this case
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n
= \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{1}{n(n+1)} = 0</m>, which means
<em>we cannot use the <m>n</m>th term test</em>. Instead let us write
the partial sum.
<md>
<mrow>s_n \amp = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{1}{k(k+1)}
= \sum_{k=1}^{n} \left(\frac1k - \frac{1}{k+1}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(\frac11 - \frac12\right)
+ \left(\frac12 - \frac13\right)
+ \left(\frac13 - \frac14\right) + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left(\frac{1}{n-1} - \frac1n\right)
+ \left(\frac1n - \frac{1}{n+1}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - \frac{1}{n+1}</mrow>
</md>
<md xml:id="eq-telescoping-sum" number="yes">
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)}
= \lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1 - \frac{1}{n+1}\right) = 1
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We begin by writing the infinite series in the sigma notation.
<md>
\frac49 - \frac{8}{27} + \frac{16}{81} - \frac{32}{243} + \cdots
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac49\left(-\frac23\right)^{n-1}
</md>
Note that <em>the <m>n</m>th Term Test is not conclusive</em> for this
one, since <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = 0</m>. However, this is a
geometric series with <m>a = \dfrac49</m> and <m>r = -\dfrac23</m>, so
<m>|r| \lt 1</m> and <xref ref="eq-geometric-sum"/> applies.
<md>
<mrow>\frac49 - \frac{8}{27} + \frac{16}{81} - \frac{32}{243} + \cdots
\amp = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac49\left(-\frac23\right)^{n-1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\frac49}{1-\left(-\frac23\right)}
= \frac{4}{15}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-series-combining">
<title>Combining Series</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-series-combining">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\sum a_n = A</m> and <m>\sum b_n = B</m> are convergent series,
then

<ol>
<li>
<p>
<em>Sum Rule:</em>
<m>\sum (a_n + b_n) = \sum a_n + \sum b_n = A + B</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Difference Rule:</em>
<m>\sum (a_n - b_n) = \sum a_n - \sum b_n = A - B</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Constant Multiple Rule:</em>
<m>\sum k a_n = k \sum a_n = kA</m> (Any number <m>k</m>).
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-series-difference">
<title>Using the difference rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\frac{1}{n(n+1)} - \frac{1}{2^n}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that in <xref ref="eq-telescoping-sum"/> and
<xref ref="eq-halving-sum"/> we already found that

<md>
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)} = 1
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n} = 1.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence, we can simply use the difference rule to conclude that

<md>
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)} - \frac{1}{2^n}
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)}
- \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n}
= 1 - 1 = 0.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-series-telescoping-ln">
<title>A telescoping series with logarithms</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\ln\!\left(\frac{k+2}{k+1}\right)</m>, if it converges and if it
diverges, show that it does.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We begin by writing the partial sum <m>s_n</m>.

<md>
<mrow>s_n \amp = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \ln\!\left(\frac{k+2}{k+1}\right)
= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \left(\ln(k+2) - \ln(k+1)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(\ln 2 - \ln 1\right)
+ \left(\ln 3 - \ln 2\right)
+ \left(\ln 4 - \ln 3\right) + \cdots</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left(\ln(n+1) - \ln(n)\right)
+ \left(\ln(n+2) - \ln(n+1)\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\ln(1) + \ln(n+2) = \ln(n+2)</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} s_n
= \lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \ln(n+2) = \infty</m>, and hence the series
diverges.
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

</section>
